So, we’d be focusing on a practical, hands-on approach for each week, where students will actively engage with tools and projects, alongside learning the essential theories in smaller, manageable doses.

Month 1: Getting Hands-On with AI Basics and Tools

Week 1: Introduction to AI and Practical Tools

Objective: Students should be able to understand the basics of AI and start using AI tools.

Activities:

Brief overview of AI (types, history, real-world applications).

Practical session: Setting up accounts and exploring basic AI tools like ChatGPT, DALL·E, or Bard.

Assignment/Milestone: Students will generate simple text or image outputs using AI tools and reflect on their experiences.



Week 2: Exploring Types of AI and Simple Applications

Objective: Students should be able to differentiate between various types of AI and create simple AI-driven applications.

Activities:

Discussion on Narrow AI, General AI, and Super AI.

Practical: Building a simple chatbot using Dialogflow or a similar tool.

Assignment/Milestone: Deploy the chatbot and share the link, detailing how it works.



Week 3: Introduction to Prompt Engineering

Objective: Students should be able to craft effective prompts for AI tools to get desired outcomes.

Activities:

Theory: What is prompt engineering and why is it important?

Workshop: Hands-on with various AI models to test different prompts.

Assignment/Milestone: Create a guide on effective prompts for different tasks (e.g., writing, designing).



Week 4: AI in Content Creation

Objective: Students should be able to use AI tools for creating digital content.

Activities:

Brief on AI in media and creative industries.

Practical: Using AI tools like MidJourney for graphics and Jasper for writing content.

Assignment/Milestone: Create a piece of digital content (e.g., blog post, graphic) using AI tools and publish it.




---

Month 2: Diving Deeper into AI Applications

Week 5: AI for Developers – Coding with AI

Objective: Students should be able to use AI tools to assist in coding and debugging.

Activities:

Introduction to AI-powered IDEs and tools like GitHub Copilot.

Practical: Solve a coding problem with AI assistance.

Assignment/Milestone: Submit a code snippet improved by AI suggestions and explain the process.


Week 6: AI in Cybersecurity

Objective: Students should be able to understand and apply AI in cybersecurity contexts.

Activities:

Discuss AI's role in threat detection and prevention.

Practical: Simulate a basic threat detection system using AI tools.

Assignment/Milestone: Create a report on the effectiveness of AI in a simulated cybersecurity scenario.



Week 7: Building AI-Powered Applications

Objective: Students should be able to build simple AI-powered applications.

Activities:

Introduction to APIs (e.g., OpenAI API) and how to integrate them into apps.

Practical: Develop a basic AI-powered web application.

Assignment/Milestone: Deploy the application and provide a demonstration.



Week 8: Midterm Project

Objective: Apply all learned concepts in a collaborative project.

Project Task: In teams, develop an AI-powered solution for a chosen problem.

Milestone: Present a working prototype and document the development process.



---

Month 3: Advanced Tools and Capstone Project

Week 9: Exploring Advanced AI Tools and Platforms

Objective: Students should be able to use advanced AI tools for various applications.

Activities:

Introduction to advanced tools like Hugging Face, TensorFlow Lite.

Practical: Hands-on with these tools to create more complex applications.

Assignment/Milestone: Develop a small project using an advanced AI tool and present the outcomes.



Week 10: AI Deployment and Real-World Applications

Objective: Students should be able to deploy AI models and understand their real-world impact.

Activities:

Overview of deployment techniques using platforms like Vercel or Heroku.

Practical: Deploy a simple AI model and test its functionality.

Assignment/Milestone: Provide a live demo of the deployed model and a write-up on its potential real-world uses.



Week 11: Preparing for the Final Project

Objective: Plan and prepare for a comprehensive final project.

Activities:

Workshop on project planning and execution.

Group discussions to finalize project ideas and tasks.

Milestone: Submit a detailed project proposal outlining objectives, tools, and expected outcomes.



Week 12: Final Project Presentation

Objective: Showcase the final project and reflect on the learning journey.

Project Task: Complete the final project and present it to the class.

Milestone: Deliver a presentation, including a demo of the project, and submit a final report.



---

This structure ensures a balance between theory and practice, emphasizing hands-on learning through engaging assignments and projects.


